{"code":0,"data":{"title":"Asynchronous I/O on Linux","content":"# Asynchronous I/O on Linux\n\nIn systems programming, the I/O (input/output) model determines how you interact with data, and it has a critical impact on overall system performance. The I/O model directly affects several major areas:\n\n* Throughput\n* Machine resource utilization, e.g. CPU usage\n* System scalability\n* Programming complexity\n\nIn network programming, the I/O model is the foundation of a high-performance server. If it's poorly designed, no matter how elegant the upper layers are, everything stalls at the I/O layer and becomes the performance bottleneck.\n\nThis post focuses on two core I/O models on Linux—synchronous and asynchronous I/O—and explains them alongside the relevant socket functions.\n\n## Part 1: Synchronous I/O—the familiar \"blocking\" and \"non-blocking\"[ #](#part-1-synchronous-iothe-familiar-blocking-and-non-blocking)\n\nIn plain terms, synchronous I/O means: after the caller initiates an I/O operation, it must wait for the data to be ready before it can continue.\n\n### Core sub-model 1: Blocking I/O[ #](#core-sub-model-1-blocking-io)\n\nIn Linux, a socket created with `socket()` is in blocking mode by default. In this mode, when you call `recv()`, `read()`, or `accept()` and the data isn't ready, the process is suspended into a blocked state until the data becomes ready, at which point the process resumes.\n\n> \"Process suspension\" here means thread suspension in a multithreaded scenario.\n\nThis approach has pros and cons. The upside is that it's simple to program—the logic is intuitive and easy to understand. The downside is low efficiency: especially under high concurrency, a single thread can handle only one connection, which fails to make full use of the CPU.\n\n### Core sub-model 2: Non-blocking I/O[ #](#core-sub-model-2-non-blocking-io)\n\nOn Linux, non-blocking I/O is enabled by using `fcntl()` to put the socket into non-blocking mode. Once that takes effect, if the data isn't ready the caller immediately gets an error return (usually `EAGAIN` or `EWOULDBLOCK`); the calling thread isn't suspended and can go do other work.\n\nThe advantage here is obvious: the thread doesn't block, so it can handle many more tasks and make much better use of the CPU. But the downside is equally clear: it needs some kind of continuous polling to check whether the I/O data is ready, which also burns CPU.\n\n### A summary of the two modes[ #](#a-summary-of-the-two-modes)\n\nThe essence of synchronous I/O is that **after issuing an I/O request, the caller must wait for the data copy to complete**. Blocking and non-blocking are just different strategies for the stage of waiting for the data to become ready.\n\n## Part 2: Asynchronous I/O—let the OS handle I/O for you[ #](#part-2-asynchronous-iolet-the-os-handle-io-for-you)\n\nIn Part 1 we saw that blocking I/O has low CPU utilization, while non-blocking I/O is complex to control and its mechanism also consumes a lot of CPU. To solve these problems more effectively, OS-level asynchronous I/O was introduced.\n\nThe flow is as follows: after the caller initiates the I/O operation, it can return immediately and continue with other tasks. When the I/O operation **completes**, the OS notifies the caller through some mechanism (such as a callback). It's like ordering takeout—you can keep working, and you get a notification when it arrives.\n\nThis way, the OS solves the control complexity and CPU consumption of non-blocking I/O for us.\n\nHere we introduce Linux's asynchronous I/O mechanisms. Note that they are meant for reading disk block devices, not for network I/O; the goal is to illustrate the idea of asynchronous I/O on Linux. Asynchronous I/O breaks down into the following steps:\n\n### Step 1: Issue the asynchronous I/O request[ #](#step-1-issue-the-asynchronous-io-request)\n\nThe flow for issuing asynchronous I/O:\n\n1. The application calls a special asynchronous I/O request function (e.g. `aio_read()` or `io_submit()`).\n2. This call immediately submits the I/O request (including the file descriptor to read, the buffer address, the data size, and so on) to the **kernel**.\n3. This is the key step. The submission function **returns immediately** rather than waiting for the data read/write to complete. This means the application can keep doing other tasks without having to stop.\n\n> LibAIO vs. Linux native asynchronous I/O functions\n>\n> LibAIO is POSIX-compliant and has better cross-platform support, but it actually simulates asynchrony using a thread pool, so its performance is not ideal. Typical functions:\n>\n> * `aio_read()`\n> * `aio_write()`\n> * `aio_fsync()`\n> * `aio_error()`\n> * `aio_return()`\n> * `aio_suspend()`\n> * `aio_cancel()`\n> * `aio_listio()`\n>\n> Linux's native asynchronous I/O functions are a more direct and efficient way to interact with the kernel. They submit I/O requests directly into the kernel's event queue instead of relying on thread simulation, achieving true kernel-level asynchrony. Their main drawback is that they only run on Linux and are not portable. Typical functions:\n>\n> * `io_setup()`\n> * `io_getevents()`\n> * `io_submit()`\n> * `io_cancel()`\n> * `io_destroy()`\n\n### Step 2: The kernel takes over and processes the I/O[ #](#step-2-the-kernel-takes-over-and-processes-the-io)\n\nOnce the request is submitted, the rest of the work is done by the kernel, and the application hardly needs to worry about it.\n\n* **Kernel scheduling**: After receiving the request, the kernel puts it in a queue and schedules an appropriate time to handle it.\n* **Hardware interaction**: The kernel interacts with the hardware (such as the disk controller) to start the data transfer. This happens entirely in the background and usually requires no CPU involvement (via **DMA, Direct Memory Access**).\n* **Data copy**: Data is copied directly from the hardware device into the memory buffer specified by the application. The copy is handled entirely by the kernel, with no application involvement.\n\n### Step 3: The I/O operation completes and notifies[ #](#step-3-the-io-operation-completes-and-notifies)\n\nOnce the copy is done, the kernel needs to notify the application in some way to tell it \"your I/O task is complete.\"\n\n**Notification mechanism**: This is the heart of the asynchronous I/O model. There are several ways to notify:\n\n* **Signal**: The kernel can send the application a signal (e.g. `SIGIO`) to indicate the I/O task is complete. The application must register a signal handler in advance to catch it.\n* **Callback function**: This is the more common way. When issuing the request, the application provides the address of a callback. When the I/O completes, the kernel **calls it automatically**, passing the result (such as success or failure) as an argument.\n\n### Step 4: The application handles the result[ #](#step-4-the-application-handles-the-result)\n\nOnce the application receives the completion notification, it can safely process the data.\n\n* **Check the result**: After receiving the notification, the application checks the result of the operation, such as the number of bytes read or whether an error occurred.\n* **Continue processing**: The buffer now holds the complete data, so the application can start parsing, processing, or responding to it.\n\n## Part 3: I/O multiplexing—the dawn of asynchrony in a synchronous world[ #](#part-3-io-multiplexingthe-dawn-of-asynchrony-in-a-synchronous-world)\n\nThe two parts above introduced non-blocking I/O and asynchronous I/O programming. We saw that non-blocking is inefficient while asynchronous I/O programming is fairly complex. Is there a middle ground? Yes—**I/O multiplexing**.\n\nI/O multiplexing means a single thread can simultaneously watch multiple `socket` file descriptors; when an event (data readable or writable) occurs on one of them, the kernel notifies the application layer.\n\nHere we cover just a few core APIs:\n\n* **`select()`**: The earliest API. Its advantage is cross-platform support; its drawbacks are that performance degrades linearly with the number of `socket`s and there is a limit on the number of file descriptors.\n* **`poll()`**: An improved version of `select()` that removes the file-descriptor count limit.\n* **`epoll()`**: A high-performance API unique to Linux. It has two modes—**edge-triggered (ET)** and **level-triggered (LT)**—and it excels at large-scale concurrent connections because it returns only the descriptors that are already ready, without polling all of them.\n\nI/O multiplexing **is still fundamentally synchronous I/O** (because after the kernel notifies it, the application layer still has to issue its own `read()` or `write()` to copy the data). But by **waiting for multiple file descriptors to become ready**, it solves both the efficiency problem of blocking I/O and the polling problem of non-blocking I/O, achieving the efficient \"one thread handling many connections\" pattern.\n\n## Part 4: Summary and choices[ #](#part-4-summary-and-choices)\n\nChoosing among **blocking I/O**, **non-blocking I/O**, **I/O multiplexing**, and **asynchronous I/O** requires weighing several factors—programming complexity, performance, applicable scenarios, and so on—to pick the approach that fits your business.\n\nGeneral recommendations:\n\n* **Blocking I/O**: Suitable for client applications and servers with few connections and modest performance requirements.\n* **I/O multiplexing (especially `epoll`)**: The **first choice for high-concurrency servers**, such as web servers and chat rooms. It strikes the best balance between programming complexity and performance.\n* **Asynchronous I/O**: Suitable when you need extreme I/O performance and can accept the complexity of the programming model, such as databases and high-performance storage systems.\n\nI/O model technology keeps advancing; a notable example is the arrival of `io_uring` on Linux, which can support all types of I/O, including network I/O.\n","locale":"en","slug":"linuxsystemasyncio"}}